Fixed in `src/attr/_make.py:619-627` (`_make_cached_property_getattr`): when the class has no original `__getattr__`, the generated fallback now does

```python
try:
    return super().__getattribute__(item)
except AttributeError:
    if not hasattr(super(), '__getattr__'):
        raise
    return super().__getattr__(item)
```

so an `AttributeError` raised inside a cached property's body (or by a plain `@property` it reads) propagates unchanged, while a base-class `__getattr__` is still consulted when one exists and genuinely missing attributes still yield the standard `'A' object has no attribute 'z'` message from `object.__getattribute__`.

